<h1>Markov chain</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A <a href="finiteStateMachine.html"><em>finite state machine</em></a> with probabilities for each transition, that is, a probability that the next state is s<sub>j</sub> given that the current state is s<sub>i</sub>.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="hiddenMarkovModel.html"><em>hidden Markov model</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
Equivalently, a <a href="weightedDigraph.html"><em>weighted, directed graph</em></a> in which the weights correspond to the probability of that transition.  In other words, the weights are nonnegative and the total weight of outgoing <a href="edge.html"><em>edges</em></a> is positive.  If the weights are normalized, the total weight, including <a href="selfloop.html"><em>self-loops</em></a>, is 1. After <a href="../terms.html#Leda98">[Leda98]</a>.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>
<h2>More information</h2>
<p>
Named after <a href="https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Markov/" target="_blank">Andrei Andreyevich Markov</a> (1856 - 1922), who studied poetry and other texts as stochastic sequences of characters.
</p>

